<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jaya Prakash Malla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1768">1768</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jaya Prakash Malla was the last Malla king of Kantipur (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>), ruling with interruptions from 1736 to 1768. His reign was troubled by quarrels with his nobles and with the other Malla kingdoms of the valley, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> of Gorkha blockaded the valley. In 1768 Prithvi Narayan captured Kathmandu during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra-jatra">Indra Jatra</a> festival, and Jaya Prakash fled, dying soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jaya-prakash-malla.jpg" alt="Jaya Prakash Malla" width="320" height="534" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nyeta · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miniature_of_Jaya_Prakash_Malla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Jaya Prakash Malla succeeded his father, Jagajjaya Malla, in 1736. His elder brother Rajendra Malla, the heir, had died young, and some courtiers had asked the king to name a younger brother, Rajyaprakash, instead; his feud with the nobles began when the king chose Jaya Prakash. Suspecting a coup, he expelled Rajyaprakash during the mourning for their father, and Vishnu Malla, King of Patan, adopted Rajyaprakash as his heir. When palace officials proclaimed another brother, Narendraprakash, ruler of the north-east of the kingdom, Jaya Prakash defeated him after four months.</p><p>In 1737 he quickly defeated an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuwakot">Nuwakot</a> by Narabhupal Shah of Gorkha, but in 1744 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> attacked Nuwakot again and annexed it. In 1746 his queen, Daya Lakshmi, allied with the nobility to depose him and install their eighteen-month-old son, Jyoti Prakasa. Jaya Prakash lived in exile, reported by the Capuchin missionaries to be hiding in caves and huts of straw and sticks, until with the help of his mother, Kumudini Devi, and as Daya Lakshmi lost the nobles' favour, he was restored on 30 April 1750.</p><p>In 1753 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capuchins">Capuchins</a> reported that he imprisoned Daya Lakshmi on a charge of adultery and had her lover cut to pieces before her. His son Jyoti Prakasa died on 27 April 1763. His rule ended with his defeat by the Shahs on 25 September 1768; he sought asylum with Tej Narasimha Malla in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur">Lalitpur</a> and then fled with him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He wrote in Classical Newar, among his works Padma Samuchaya and three dramas on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> legends, Ratneshwar Pradurbhav, Birdhwojopakhyan Natakam and Bhairavpradurbhav. In Ratneshwar Pradurbhav, dated to around 1745, many poems express his love for Daya Lakshmi, and he signed some poems as the lord of Daya Lakshmi. He also started several of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>'s important festivals, among them the Kumari Jatra, and contributed to the city's architecture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Judgements of him differ. The historian Dilli Raman Regmi described an overbearing manner, haughty temper and suspicious mind, and held him largely responsible for the Gorkhali conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. The British traveller Perceval Landon saw a capable and valiant but headstrong ruler who could not keep his alliances with the other kings of Nepal. Prem Bahadur Kansakar and Janak Lal Vaidya, reading his plays, found in them an awareness of his own flaws and concern for his people, and Oldfield, physician to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jang-bahadur-rana">Jung Bahadur Rana</a>, called him a gallant ruler who resisted the Gorkhali invasion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jaya Prakash Malla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A small British expedition under Captain George Kinloch, sent in 1767 to help the Newar king <b>Jaya Prakash Malla</b>, had already failed in the Terai lowlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Prithvi Narayan Shah of the Gorkha Kingdom captured Kathmandu during the Indra Jatra festival in 1768, while <b>Jaya Prakash Malla</b> fled.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kantipur">Kingdom of Kantipur</a></li></ul></section>
